% Generate `web/src/chat/chatUtils.js`: tiny, pure chat helpers shared by the agent
% hook (`useAgentChat`) and the chat view (`ChatPanel`), so both the pipeline window
% and the editor can import them. No React, no side effects — just `export`s.

## Model dropdown
`AGENT_MODELS`: exported array of `{ id, label, recommended? }` for the chat-header
model picker; the first entry is the default and the only one with `recommended: true`.
Ids MUST match `server/agent_runner.py` AGENT_MODELS (the backend validates against them):
- `claude-opus-4-8` — "Opus 4.8" (recommended)
- `claude-sonnet-5` — "Sonnet 5"
- `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` — "Haiku 4.5"

`DEFAULT_MODEL` = `AGENT_MODELS[0].id`.

## Tool icons
`TOOL_ICON`: map from tool name → icon component (imported from `../components/icons.jsx`,
rendered by ChatPanel's ActivityChip): Read→IconFileText; Write/Edit/MultiEdit→IconPencil;
Bash→IconTerminal; Glob/Grep→IconSearch; WebSearch/WebFetch→IconWorld; Skill→IconTools;
TodoWrite→IconListCheck; render & mcp__mc__render→IconMovie.

## isRenderCommand(item)
True iff `item` is a render-in-progress `tool_use` (drives the inline render progress bar,
which appears on tool_use and clears on the matching tool_result). False unless
`item.kind === 'tool_use'`. True for the in-process render tool (`name` is `mcp__mc__render`
or `render`); otherwise true only for a `Bash` whose lowercased `detail` mentions any of
`npx remotion`, `ffmpeg`, `npm run render`, or `hyperframes render`.

## formatToolInput(item)
Pretty-print a tool_use `input` (`item.input || {}`) for the expandable activity chip:
- `Bash` → `input.command || ''`.
- `Write`/`Edit`/`MultiEdit`/`NotebookEdit`: let `path = input.file_path || input.path || ''`;
  if `input.content != null` return `` `${path}\n\n${input.content}` ``; else if
  `input.old_string` or `input.new_string` is non-null return a diff block
  `` `${path}\n\n- - - old - - -\n${old_string}\n\n+ + + new + + +\n${new_string}` ``
  (each string defaulting to `''`); else just `path`.
- anything else → `JSON.stringify(input, null, 2)`.
